WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:05.139
Welcome to another Epic Games Unreal Engine training course.

2
00:00:05.139 --> 00:00:08.140
Today we're going to be taking a look at an introduction to animation

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.650
in Unreal Engine version 5.5.

4
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:15.650
So we're going to aim to end with an understanding of the different types of animations

5
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.649
that you have within the engine.

6
00:00:17.649 --> 00:00:21.649
And we'll take a look at the animation editors and the different features that are available

7
00:00:21.649 --> 00:00:24.649
depending on what type of animation you're working with,

8
00:00:24.649 --> 00:00:28.649
how you can import and export animations to and from the engine.

9
00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:33.649
We'll also get an understanding of the differences between skeletons and skeletal meshes

10
00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:36.649
and cover a brief overview of the animation subeditors.

11
00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:40.649
Then we'll go over how to create some new animation assets within the engine

12
00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:44.649
and for that we'll utilize Sequencer and pre-existing animations

13
00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:50.899
and we'll briefly cover how to attach an item to a character using a socket.

14
00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:54.899
So when we're talking about the different types of animation that you can have in engine

15
00:00:54.899 --> 00:00:56.899
what do we mean by this?

16
00:00:56.899 --> 00:01:02.899
So it's more understanding what you're going to use that animation for

17
00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:06.900
which will sort of dictate how you should approach creating the animation

18
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.599
and how you set the animation up in engine.

19
00:01:10.599 --> 00:01:16.599
The first type of animation you could consider is what we might call input animation.

20
00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:21.599
So these animations are typically used for the player character that you're controlling

21
00:01:21.599 --> 00:01:24.599
and they're authored on the spot so the character won't be moving through space

22
00:01:24.599 --> 00:01:26.599
when you export these animations.

23
00:01:26.599 --> 00:01:32.599
Now the reason for that is that it's not the animations that dictate the character's movement

24
00:01:32.599 --> 00:01:34.599
but something called the player pawn.

25
00:01:34.599 --> 00:01:38.599
Now the player pawn is essentially a container of logic

26
00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:43.599
that takes the input information from your controller or your keyboard or your iPad

27
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:47.599
or whatever it might be that you're using to control the character

28
00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:52.629
and it dictates what the character should be doing when you press a certain button.

29
00:01:52.629 --> 00:01:56.950
So when I press W on my keyboard the character's moving forwards

30
00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.950
and when I press S it's moving backwards.

31
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:05.180
And you can see this sort of container around our character here

32
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.180
something that's called the capsule.

33
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:13.270
Now this is just a visual representation of where this character's moving and how they're moving.

34
00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.270
So as I traverse around the space you can see that that container is moving

35
00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.270
and we've got animations playing within that container.

36
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:26.879
Now it's the animation blueprint itself that dictates what animation should be playing on the character

37
00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.879
and it sort of gives that illusion of movement.

38
00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:32.879
So as this capsule is moving through the world

39
00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:34.879
these animations are playing on the spot

40
00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:37.879
at a speed that matches the capsule's speed

41
00:02:37.879 --> 00:02:39.879
to give that illusion of movement.

42
00:02:39.879 --> 00:02:43.419
And this can be done with all sorts of different animation types

43
00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:47.419
so if you're jumping, you're running or walking, you're flying or swimming

44
00:02:47.419 --> 00:02:49.419
you know whatever it is you want your character to do

45
00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:57.520
you'd first set up the inputs from the controller to the player pawn

46
00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.520
and then that information is fed through to the animation blueprint

47
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.520
which tells the character what animation to be playing

48
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.520
and how it should look when it's moving in a specific way.

49
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:13.569
And the other type of animation we would call linear animation.

50
00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:17.569
So this is more often used for cinematics

51
00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:21.569
or something that doesn't need to be controlled at runtime essentially.

52
00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:25.569
And also this means it doesn't need to be authored on the spot.

53
00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:28.569
So this is where our character or whatever it might be

54
00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:30.569
can be moving through space in the world

55
00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:34.699
and not worry about having to make adjustments at runtime.

56
00:03:34.699 --> 00:03:39.699
So this could come in the form of animations that you've created in external software

57
00:03:39.699 --> 00:03:42.699
or you could actually create them directly in the engine

58
00:03:42.699 --> 00:03:44.699
using Control Rig inside Sequencer

59
00:03:44.699 --> 00:03:48.699
or maybe you've got pre-recorded mocap or alembic caches

60
00:03:48.699 --> 00:03:50.699
that you could also set up inside Sequencer.

61
00:03:50.699 --> 00:03:54.789
But the key point is that they don't need to be controlled at runtime

62
00:03:54.789 --> 00:03:57.789
and it's something that doesn't need to be authored on the spot

63
00:03:57.789 --> 00:03:59.789
the same way that an input animation needs to be.

64
00:03:59.789 --> 00:04:04.789
It's more like a one shot or a cinematic that would trigger once

65
00:04:04.789 --> 00:04:06.789
and then you would just allow that to play out.

66
00:04:06.789 --> 00:04:13.099
Digging a little deeper into how animations are set up in engine

67
00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:15.330
we have something called root motion.

68
00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.329
Now earlier I said that it's not the animation

69
00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.329
that drives the movement of the character

70
00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.329
but it's actually the movement of the character

71
00:04:22.329 --> 00:04:25.329
that defines what animation should be being played.

72
00:04:25.329 --> 00:04:29.360
So whether that's walking or jumping or running, whatever it is.

73
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.360
Now root motion is a way of actually flipping that relationship.

74
00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.360
And as you can see here, it's a method to control the character's movement

75
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.360
using the animation data on the skeleton's root bone.

76
00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.899
So looking at this GIF that's playing on the right hand side

77
00:04:44.899 --> 00:04:46.899
you can see that as the animation plays

78
00:04:46.899 --> 00:04:49.899
the character is moving forward through space

79
00:04:49.899 --> 00:04:51.899
and as they return to that idle pose

80
00:04:51.899 --> 00:04:54.899
you can see that they blend back to the capsule

81
00:04:54.899 --> 00:04:59.220
because they're currently exiting the capsule with the animation.

82
00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.220
Now when you enable a root motion inside the animation

83
00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.220
you can see on the left hand side here

84
00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:08.220
that that information on the root is being transferred to the capsule

85
00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.220
and as they're blending back to idle they're not snapping back

86
00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.220
because the capsule is now moved to where that root would have been.

87
00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.410
Now an important thing to note here

88
00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.410
is that a skeleton needs a root bone for this to work.

89
00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:26.410
So if you're downloading animations or skeletons from somewhere online

90
00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:28.410
like Mixamo for example

91
00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.410
you might find that they don't actually have a root bone

92
00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:35.410
or they're using like the hips or pelvis in place of it

93
00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.410
and it's actually in the wrong place.

94
00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.439
So what you need is a root bone, a unique bone

95
00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:42.439
that's at the top of the hierarchy

96
00:05:42.439 --> 00:05:45.439
that is at world zero by default.

97
00:05:45.439 --> 00:05:48.209
A second thing to note is that

98
00:05:48.209 --> 00:05:51.209
root motion is not enabled by default.

99
00:05:51.209 --> 00:05:54.209
So this is something that you need to enable in the animation itself

100
00:05:54.209 --> 00:05:57.209
and then something you need to set up in the animation blueprint

101
00:05:57.209 --> 00:06:01.209
to take the data from the skeleton's root bone

102
00:06:01.209 --> 00:06:03.209
and transfer that to the capsule

103
00:06:03.209 --> 00:06:05.209
so that you can get the movement from it

104
00:06:05.209 --> 00:06:08.209
and use that when you're controlling the character.

105
00:06:08.209 --> 00:06:10.100
To give an example of this

106
00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.100
you can see that we've got a root bone at the top here

107
00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.100
the top of all of our hierarchy

108
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.100
which is essentially your reference point

109
00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.100
for the entire skeletal structure

110
00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.100
and so everything would be a child of this

111
00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.170
in one way or another.

112
00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:27.170
Now in characters like bipeds or quadrupeds

113
00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:30.170
or anything that's living typically

114
00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:33.170
the root goes to sort of a central branching point

115
00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.170
that is typically the pelvis or the hips

116
00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:38.170
and then everything else branches off that

117
00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:41.170
in smaller branches like your spine

118
00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:43.170
your hands down to your fingers

119
00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:46.259
or your thigh down to your feet.

120
00:06:46.259 --> 00:06:48.259
And what you'll find is with something like a car

121
00:06:48.259 --> 00:06:51.259
or something mechanical that's non-organic

122
00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:54.259
you may actually have several of these branching points

123
00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:57.259
depending on the requirement of the thing that you're animating

124
00:06:57.259 --> 00:07:01.259
or what the mesh needs to do.

125
00:07:01.259 --> 00:07:06.509
Now organic characters often require things like twist joints as well.

126
00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:09.769
Now these allow you to preserve the volume

127
00:07:09.769 --> 00:07:12.769
of like forearm for example as the wrist twists

128
00:07:12.769 --> 00:07:15.769
and the recommended hierarchy for these

129
00:07:15.769 --> 00:07:18.769
are that they're best created as individual children

130
00:07:18.769 --> 00:07:20.769
of for example the elbow

131
00:07:20.769 --> 00:07:23.769
rather than being in a direct chain

132
00:07:23.769 --> 00:07:25.769
from the elbow to the wrist.

133
00:07:25.769 --> 00:07:28.769
Now this just gives you more control over the rotation

134
00:07:28.769 --> 00:07:30.769
without affecting the other joints in the chain.

135
00:07:30.769 --> 00:07:34.379
And overall the key to the successful skeleton

136
00:07:34.379 --> 00:07:36.379
is just the hierarchy

137
00:07:36.379 --> 00:07:39.379
and considering how your character needs to deform

138
00:07:39.379 --> 00:07:43.459
and setting up the hierarchy and service of that.

139
00:07:43.459 --> 00:07:45.459
So we've looked quite a bit at gameplay animations

140
00:07:45.459 --> 00:07:47.459
and how you should set up your skeleton

141
00:07:47.459 --> 00:07:49.459
and the reasons why you might animate something

142
00:07:49.459 --> 00:07:51.459
on the spot or whatever.

143
00:07:51.459 --> 00:07:53.459
But now if we compare sort of

144
00:07:53.459 --> 00:07:56.459
cinematic animation pipelines

145
00:07:56.459 --> 00:07:58.939
sort of in engine and out of engine

146
00:07:58.939 --> 00:08:01.939
you'll find that a pre-made animation pipeline

147
00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:03.939
you know it's a fairly rigid workflow.

148
00:08:03.939 --> 00:08:05.939
It's been developed over many years

149
00:08:05.939 --> 00:08:07.939
by many different companies.

150
00:08:07.939 --> 00:08:09.939
You know typically you'll create

151
00:08:09.939 --> 00:08:11.939
and you'll animate your characters

152
00:08:11.939 --> 00:08:13.939
in an external software like Maya or Blender

153
00:08:13.939 --> 00:08:15.939
and then you'll bring those baked

154
00:08:15.939 --> 00:08:17.939
animations into the engine

155
00:08:17.939 --> 00:08:19.939
where you would then you know

156
00:08:19.939 --> 00:08:21.939
set up the logic to utilize those

157
00:08:21.939 --> 00:08:23.939
animations. So typically in

158
00:08:23.939 --> 00:08:25.939
Sequencer the cinematic tool in engine

159
00:08:25.939 --> 00:08:27.939
you'd take those baked

160
00:08:27.939 --> 00:08:29.939
animation assets and you could

161
00:08:29.939 --> 00:08:31.939
maybe stitch some animations

162
00:08:31.939 --> 00:08:33.940
back to back blend them with one another

163
00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.940
even apply some animations

164
00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.940
additively to get like some facial

165
00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:39.940
animation on there or

166
00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.940
some other additive animation

167
00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.940
on there. But any

168
00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.940
animation any edit you need to make

169
00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:47.940
really you tend to come

170
00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.940
back into your external software

171
00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:51.940
make that edit export

172
00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:53.940
your animation and then import it back

173
00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.940
into the engine.

174
00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:58.200
Now you can

175
00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.200
modify the animation in Sequencer

176
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:02.200
with the FK control rig that

177
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.200
comes with it. So you could bake to the control rig and

178
00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.200
then make your edits and then bake that back down to

179
00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.200
an animation sequence.

180
00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.389
But typically what you would

181
00:09:10.389 --> 00:09:12.389
do is you would go back to your

182
00:09:12.389 --> 00:09:14.389
external software make your edits and bring that

183
00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:17.799
back into the engine. And here's

184
00:09:17.799 --> 00:09:19.799
an example of you know that typical pipeline.

185
00:09:19.799 --> 00:09:21.860
It's quite like an

186
00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.860
assembly line where one thing

187
00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:25.860
typically needs to be completed before the next

188
00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.860
thing can start. So kicking

189
00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:29.860
off with your story development your story

190
00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.860
boards getting those

191
00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.860
iterated on and then going

192
00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:35.860
back and forth with the tutorial and

193
00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.860
as soon as they get signed off

194
00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.860
that then allows you to start your modeling and

195
00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:41.860
your rigging and your you know your

196
00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.860
scene set up.

197
00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:45.860
And as soon as that's all worked on and that's set up

198
00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.860
and that's been signed off you can then

199
00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:49.860
start animating so you can start blocking

200
00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.860
in your rough layout of your

201
00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:53.860
you know your characters animation

202
00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:55.860
and then you'll keep

203
00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.860
iterating back and forth back and forth back and

204
00:09:57.860 --> 00:09:59.860
forth until that's signed off. And once

205
00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:01.860
that's when the next the next team

206
00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.860
can kind of jump on it and start working on

207
00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:05.860
maybe it's effects or crowds

208
00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.860
or moving on to

209
00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.860
lighting after that. But you'll see

210
00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.860
that typically one

211
00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.860
thing can't really start until the

212
00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.860
previous has been completed

213
00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.889
and comparing that to

214
00:10:18.889 --> 00:10:20.889
the pipeline in engine you have

215
00:10:20.889 --> 00:10:22.889
a lot of tools that will allow you to keep the rigging

216
00:10:22.889 --> 00:10:24.889
and the animation inside the engine

217
00:10:24.889 --> 00:10:26.889
without the need for re exporting

218
00:10:26.889 --> 00:10:28.889
and then re importing when you need to make some

219
00:10:28.889 --> 00:10:31.370
changes. So tools within

220
00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:33.370
the engine like control rig

221
00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:35.370
and I've got access to

222
00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:37.370
procedural rigging and modular rigging

223
00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:39.370
and these allow you to

224
00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:41.370
create templates for different parts of

225
00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.370
the skeletons that work instantly

226
00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:45.370
across multiple characters in the project

227
00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:47.370
so you could create and set

228
00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:49.370
up sort of FK and IK arms

229
00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:51.370
and legs that you can instantly just plug

230
00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:53.370
into different characters and that will just work straight

231
00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.429
away. You can then animate

232
00:10:55.429 --> 00:10:57.429
directly in the scene that you're rendering

233
00:10:57.429 --> 00:10:59.429
in with your control rig inside the sequencer

234
00:10:59.429 --> 00:11:01.429
so this allows you to

235
00:11:01.429 --> 00:11:03.429
work with other teams in parallel as the scenes

236
00:11:03.429 --> 00:11:05.429
are being built and you're not having to wait

237
00:11:05.429 --> 00:11:07.429
for them to be finished before you can start

238
00:11:07.429 --> 00:11:09.429
before you can start animating.

239
00:11:09.429 --> 00:11:12.139
Some of the newer tools in Unreal

240
00:11:12.139 --> 00:11:14.139
Engine as well actually allow you

241
00:11:14.139 --> 00:11:16.139
to create skeletons

242
00:11:16.139 --> 00:11:18.139
and skin characters directly

243
00:11:18.139 --> 00:11:20.139
in the engine. This is available

244
00:11:20.139 --> 00:11:22.139
from version 5.3 and

245
00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:24.139
above which means that your

246
00:11:24.139 --> 00:11:26.139
entire rigging and animation process can live

247
00:11:26.139 --> 00:11:28.139
inside the engine working with the assets

248
00:11:28.139 --> 00:11:30.139
and the final scene that you're rendering out.

249
00:11:30.139 --> 00:11:32.970
A real-time pipeline has

250
00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.970
a few benefits over the offline pipeline

251
00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:36.970
or the pre-made pipeline.

252
00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:38.970
So all the previs

253
00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:40.970
can be done directly within the engine

254
00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:42.970
and then as you're working through each

255
00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:44.970
iteration you update with the final

256
00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:46.970
assets as they're being created.

257
00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:48.970
You don't have to throw away any work

258
00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:50.970
and everything that you're doing is building

259
00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:52.970
towards that final shot through each iteration.

260
00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:55.029
Now that allows teams to

261
00:11:55.029 --> 00:11:57.029
work more in parallel with one another

262
00:11:57.029 --> 00:11:59.029
rather than the waterfall effect

263
00:11:59.029 --> 00:12:01.029
where you're waiting for one to finish before you can

264
00:12:01.029 --> 00:12:03.029
kick off. You can get your characters

265
00:12:03.029 --> 00:12:05.029
rigged and set up

266
00:12:05.029 --> 00:12:07.029
and animated in scenes that are being

267
00:12:07.029 --> 00:12:09.029
set up at the same time

268
00:12:09.029 --> 00:12:11.029
and a huge advantage

269
00:12:11.029 --> 00:12:13.029
is being able to animate within the actual

270
00:12:13.029 --> 00:12:15.029
scene that you're rendering and you can see

271
00:12:15.029 --> 00:12:17.029
all the real-time lighting, how that's affecting

272
00:12:17.029 --> 00:12:19.029
your character, all the

273
00:12:19.029 --> 00:12:21.029
effects that are in there and anything that

274
00:12:21.029 --> 00:12:23.029
your character will be interacting with in the final

275
00:12:23.029 --> 00:12:25.029
shot all happening in real-time.

276
00:12:25.029 --> 00:12:27.289
And all of that together gives you

277
00:12:27.289 --> 00:12:29.289
sort of a faster iteration

278
00:12:29.289 --> 00:12:31.289
time without having to jump between different

279
00:12:31.289 --> 00:12:33.289
softwares, importing and exporting.

280
00:12:33.289 --> 00:12:35.289
You're also not having to wait for renders

281
00:12:35.289 --> 00:12:37.289
to see how your shot looks

282
00:12:37.289 --> 00:12:39.289
because it all runs in real-time

283
00:12:39.289 --> 00:12:41.289
and gives you more sort of opportunities

284
00:12:41.289 --> 00:12:43.289
to experiment and more creative

285
00:12:43.289 --> 00:12:45.289
freedom to try things that would normally

286
00:12:45.289 --> 00:12:47.289
probably be too costly or

287
00:12:47.289 --> 00:12:49.289
time consuming with that traditional linear pipeline.
